===Etymology===
Attested since 1835<ref name="Dict">{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref> as ''Kanuk'' (in US writings) and 1849 as ''canuck'' (in Canadian writings). Of {{unk|en|nocap=1}} origin, often hypothesized to derive from the name or speech of an early Canadian minority, later broadened to denote all Canadians.<ref name="Unkind">Irving Lewis Allen, ''[http://books.google.com/books?id=8PdZAAAAMAAJ&pgis=1 Unkind Words: Ethnic Labeling from Redskin to WASP],'' pages 59 and 61–62 (1990, New York: Bergin & Garvey, {{ISBN|0-89789-217-8}})</ref>
* Some dictionaries suggest it is an alteration of {{m|en|Canada}},<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref><ref name="AHD">{{R:AHD}}</ref> which in any case ensured the spelling ''Can-'',<ref name="Unkind"/> or that word's etymon {{cog|lre|kanata||village}}, or a related word ''kanuchsa'' meaning "villager" in that or another {{der|en|iro}} language.<ref>Bill Casselman, ''Casselman's Canadian Words'' (1995, {{ISBN|0773055150}})</ref>
* Some sources connect the ending to {{cog|iu|inuk||man, person}},<ref>W. W. Schuhmacher, “Once More ''Canuck''” in ''American Speech'', volume 64, number 2 (Summer), 1989, page 149</ref> from {{m|en|Chinook||Aboriginal people of the U.S. Pacific Northwest}},<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> or another First-Nation language ending like {{m|und||-uc}}, {{m|und||-uq}}, or {{m|und||-oc}}.
* Since 1975, many scholars have come to think the name is from {{der|en|haw|kanaka||man}},<ref name="Dict"/><ref name="AHD"/><ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary}}</ref> a self-appellation of indentured colonial [[canoeman|canoemen]] and Hawaiian sailors working off the Pacific Northwest, Arctic{{,}} and New England coasts,<ref name="Dollinger">Stefan Dollinger, “[http://www.let.leidenuniv.nl/hsl_shl/DCHP-2/DCHP-2/DCHP-2.htm Towards a fully revised and extended edition of the ''Dictionary of Canadianisms on Historical Principles'' (DCHP-2): background, challenges, prospects]” in ''Historical Sociolinguistics/Sociohistorical Linguisics'' (Leiden, NL), volume 6</ref> via {{cog|fr|canaque}} or (more likely) American [[whalers]]'<ref name="Dollinger"/> pidgin, and then been re-interpreted<ref name="Dict"/> as {{m|en|Canadian|Can(adian)}} + a suffix. Compare {{cog|en|Kanak}} and {{cog|fr||canaque|black person}}, {{cog|de|Kanake}}.
* Fanciful suggestions include {{cog|de||[[genug]] von Canada|enough of Canada}} (allegedly uttered by German mercenaries during the American War of Independence),<ref name="Dollinger"/> or {{cog|fr||[[quelle]] [[canule]]}} (allegedly uttered by the French during a siege of Quebec),<ref name="Dollinger"/> or the surname {{m|fr|Connaught}} {{IPAchar|/ˈkɑ.nəxt/}} (supposedly a French-Canadian nickname for the Irish).<ref name="Dollinger"/>

